======================== Eviction policies ========================

LRU
Least recently used removed.
Good general-purpose.

LFU
Least frequently used.
Better for stable hot keys.

TTL eviction
Expired first.

noeviction
Writes fail when memory full.
Critical for reliability-sensitive systems.

======================== Cache invalidation ========================

A. TTL based (Time based)
SET user:1 data EX 300
Easy but stale window exists.
Problem: Figuring out the optimal amount of time.

B. Explicit invalidation (Event based)
On update:
await db.update(...);
await redis.del(`user:${id}`);
Next read reloads fresh value.
Better than long TTLs.
Problem: At every instance where you are updating user data, you have to invalidate cache.

Correct ordering
Usually:
1. update DB
2. delete cache

NOT:
delete cache
update DB

Why?
Because concurrent readers can repopulate old data.

C. Versioning/ CAS (for race condition) 
Store version/timestamp
Only update cache if version mismatch 

D. Read your write consistency 
For few seconds force read from master DB

E. Cache aside pattern 
Update DB 
Delete from cache

======================== Cache Hit Ratio ========================

Percentage of requests served directly from cache instead of hitting the database.
Cache Hit Ratio = Cache Hits / Total Requests

Depends on:
TTL duration
Cache size
Data access patterns
Cache eviction strategy

Example:
90 cache hits out of 100 requests = 90% hit ratio

======================== Cache stampede ========================
Thundering herd problem 
Causes when:
TTL expires 
Cache is flushed 
Redis restart
Hot key gets deleted 

Cache expires.
1000 requests arrive simultaneously.
All hit DB.
DB dies.
-> thundering herd

Solutions
A. Mutex locking
Only one request rebuilds cache.
Others wait/retry.

Request comes -> tries to get lock 
If got lock:
    fetch from db
    set cache
    release lock 
else 
    wait 
    retry cache

B. Stale-while-revalidate
Serve stale data temporarily.
Background refresh.

C. Jitter TTL
All keys expire at 12:00

ttl = 300 + random(0, 60)
Prevents synchronized expiry.

D. Pre-warm cache 
Before Expiry 
    Refresh cache proactively 
Used for:
    Homepage
    Trending page 
    Config 
    Hot data 

======================== Hot keys ========================

One key gets huge traffic.

celebrity profile
flash sale product

Problem:
single Redis node bottleneck
uneven load

Solutions:
Local Cache (First level cache)
    server local memory -> redis -> db 

Replicate Hot Key (Key sharding or key splitting)
    keep the key in multiple shards 

    Instead of:
        homepage:india
    Create:
        homepage:india:1
        homepage:india:2
        homepage:india:3

    Read:
        Pick random key 
    Write case:
        Update all of the keys

Use CDN/ Edge Cache 
For: 
    Homepage 
    Trending page 
    Public data
Put it in:
    Cloudflare/ Akami/ Fastly 
So: 
    Redis is not hit at all

Logical Expiry + Background Refresh 
    Instead of letting key expire 
    One background thread refreshes it 
So:
    Redis does not get rebuild storms

Pre-warm & Protect 
For known hot keys:
    Never let them expire 
    Refresh them periodically via job 

